{"actor_ref":"claude","class":"draft_history","document_uuid":"01a1a54a-3260-73c8-936a-2b30590e860f","id":"01a1a64a-94de-747d-a7b3-7b6aa8c6e09e","idempotency_key":"f03d39506c43e23b86090495a8c0cb68466a550ec7ad537602a42447c8ed897b","occurred_at":"2026-11-04T11:21:08Z","payload":{"note":"The security-relevant finding first: MCP tools/call was calling the host DIRECTLY, so an MCP write bypassed the policy completely - no deny, no approval, nothing. It now goes through decide() with Origin::Mcp and a profile built from one setting (mcp_auto_approve, empty by default, because the design says board writes prompt for MCP and study/destructive always prompt). A write without a grant returns exactly the payload the design specifies - status: approval_required, approval_id, changeset_summary, poll: approval_status - and runs nothing: the host's call log stays empty and one approval is queued instead. approval_status is a new read-only tool so a client can poll what it queued, and it reports the request's tool, summary and state, refusing an unknown id with not_found. Schema v10 adds ai_audit, append-only, and every approval transition writes a row (approval.created, approval.approved, approval.denied); ToolResult.audit_id has carried a field since the protocol landed, and now there is a store behind it. 23 MCP tests including the three that matter here: a write without a grant queues rather than runs, a granted board write does run, and a structural write is never silently allowed. Also fixed a gate hole I had introduced the round before: crates/ai/tests/security.rs imported game/play/provider without a feature gate, so 'cargo test --workspace' could not compile it while --all-features passed - which is exactly why the Makefile runs both.","target":"t-851f"},"type":"work.note_added","v":2}
